From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sun, 25 Sep 1994 13:32:26 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-486] Functional Motivition For Evolution?

A few brief notes on evolution:

* Best modelled as a probabilistic algorithm.

* Usually involves both mutation _and_ recombination (crossover).  
Recombination is much harder to model mathematically, but many modern 
evolutionists (starting with John Maynard Smith, and now including most 
people working with genetic algorithms) consider it to be a more important
source of variation than mutation, at least in the short term
(over tens to thousands of generations).

* Due to our lack of understanding of recombination, and our lack
of sufficient characterization of the molecular dynamics of genes,
we don't yet have a full mathematical understanding of the
genetic evolutionary process.  It's a quite active research topic; 
I'm working part time on it myself due to its many parallels
with machine learning theory.  The mechanisms for transcribing
DNA to phenotype are even less well characterized, mathematically.

* "Purposefullness" comes from the relationship
between evolution and game theory.  The environment encodes "game
rules" and evolution is the differential survival of genes based on 
those rules.  Cf., for example, genetic algorithms of co-evolving players.
The computation starts only with the information encoding the rules
of the game; it often ends up with pretty good game players.  Thus,
based on variation and selection according to the game rules _alone_,
game playing _strategies_ emerge.  Until John Maynard Smith discovered
this game theory had only been applied to purposeful behavior such as
human game playing, political science, and economics.  Thus purposeful 
metaphors are often quite useful for the action of evolution, albeit 
highly prone to either criticism or abuse from those who don't understand 
in what ways they are valid.

