mutations lies. ([Evolutionary Biology] "A Boost for 'Adaptive' Mutation",
by Elizabeth Culotta)

In essence:

A British geneticist, John Cairns, found that if

	they put bacteria unable to digest lactose into a petri dish
	with only lactoe for nourishment - and found that the bacteria
	preferentially  acquired the critical mutations they needed to
	become lactose-eaters.

The same issue of Science contains two papers, "Adaptive Mutation by
Deletions in Small Mononucleotide Repeats," (S. M. Rosenburg,
S. Longerich, P. Gee, R.S. Harris) and "Adaptive Reversion of a
Frameshift Mutation in Escherichia coli by Simple Base Deletions
in Homopolymeric Runs," (P. L. Foster and J.M. Trimarchi).  The
papers outline a particular method by which organisms might change
their genetic material to meet new conditions.

Check 'em out;  Interesting stuff.
