From: levy@cs.utk.edu
Date: Sun, 25 Sep 1994 18:22:31 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-490] Possible blow to cryogencis [sic]

Doug Cutrell writes:
> 
> POSSIBLE BLOW TO CRYOGENICS:
> 
> Extropians who place a strong value in the idea of cryogenic suspension may
> want to pay careful attention to developments in this rapidly growing
> field.  If it turns out that coherent quantum fields *are* responsible for
> storing some critical state components of human consciousness, then a total
> suspension of all metabolic activity throughout the brain might result in
> the irrevocable loss of this information.  It could be that continuous
> metabolic activity must be always maintained in order to preserve important
> portions of our memories and personalities.  The obvious analogy is to the
> RAM in a computer system -- the information stored in the hardware may not
> be preserved in the event of a total loss of metabolic activity (a "power
> failure").

I think you mean "cryonics", not "cryogenics"; the latter term usually refers to
suspension of organs and tissues for transplantation and other purposes, a 
technique already in use today.

But on to your point:  I have petted live, normally behaving dogs who had
been suspended for several hours after complete body washout, at temperatures
that (I believe) were too low to support metabolic activity. I admit that dogs
may be sub-optimal example, since we don't know much about their memories or
personalities (other than through anectodes).  But the example makes
it harder for me to believe that there is some "magic" threshold of activity
below which your personality and memory disappear, never to return. 

As for computer analogies, I try to avoid them completely when talking about
any and all aspects of human behavior.  Living systems are so much more 
complicated than computers (or any other man-made machines) that comparisons
end up doing more harm than good.

-- Simon

